reply.body:
I didn't have a particular reason for removing logic from `RuleContextConstraintSemantics` specifically. The logic in there was no longer useful because we don't have a `RuleContext` to work with. We haven't constructed `RuleContext` at this point in the code. I.e. the challenge in this refactor is to keep the same logic, but without using `RuleContext`. That's why you now see previously-unnecessary logic like resolving the `target_compatible_with` labels list.

Moving all logic into `RuleContextConstraintSemantics` was not possible because it introduced circular dependencies:
```console
$ bazel test -c opt //src/test/shell/integration:target_compatible_with_test --test_output=streamed    
...
ERROR: /home/jenkins/repos/bazel/src/main/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/analysis/BUILD:156:13: in java_library rule //src/main/java/com/google/devtools/build/lib/analysis:analysis_cluster: cycle in dependency graph:
    //src/test/shell/integration:target_compatible_with_test (5b618c6fe550ca07435371dbca12d03adc0877f11b341f2c630d140c0baced48)                                               
